WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.460
Film Courage: How has the pandemic changed
the film business? Jeff Deverett, Filmmaker/College

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:11.010
Professor: Great question, very timely. The
major change is obviously the theatrical business,

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:17.650
theaters closing, so that's had a huge impact
on how movies are viewed now especially, the

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:25.369
big blockbuster tentpole releases so that's
a big one. But that also caused a huge change

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:30.279
in in the whole VOD [Video on Demand] world
like the streaming world. So all these studios

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:35.550
that were probably thinking about becoming
subscription video on demand services had

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:42.280
it in the works likely accelerated their plans
and now all of a sudden you've got eight or

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:50.020
ten major streaming services when before you
had Netflix really was it and then an Amazon

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:55.900
Prime and then of course Disney + was starting
up but Disney + really jumped in quickly and

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:01.789
then everybody followed. So I think that the
studios realized they needed another avenue

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:08.050
to exhibit their movies because they couldn't
depend on the theaters obviously and um you

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.820
know that accelerated that so much and i'm
not sure if it's good or bad i think for indie

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:18.800
films it's actually not good which i'll talk
about later but probably for overall viewership

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.950
for for consumers now there's such a vast
choice of quality programming that's available

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:30.360
at really affordable prices so i guess that's
good now me personally i love going to the

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.151
movies like i like a night out at the movie
theater i love watching you know a big movie

00:01:35.151 --> 00:01:40.110
on in a big format on a huge screen with a
great sound system you know in a very confined

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:45.380
you know like dark room like that so i for
one will be back at the movies the minute

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.250
they start up i mean because i it's a night
out for my wife and i i love watching a movie

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.700
that way hopefully a lot of people are thinking
like me and can't wait to get back to the

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.580
movies but you know a lot of people went out
and bought really big you know flat screen

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:06.280
tvs and good sound systems and you know and
i i did too and i enjoy that it's been a great

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:13.409
way to watch you know content in the comfort
of your own home but i i believe that the

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:19.219
theatrical business will be back and it will
be healthy and robust and just because human

00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:26.569
beings need to go out and need to socialize
and and that's a night out do you think things

00:02:26.569 --> 00:02:34.010
will be totally the same as they were let's
say 2019 right right toward december of 2019

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.680
once things start to open up i know we're
on the eve of it as of this interview but

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:43.379
do you think things will be returned to that
exact level or there will be changes yeah

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:48.590
i mean it's all driven obviously by the the
major studios because they have the big programming

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:54.200
that people want to see so i don't know just
because there's going to be a decision made

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.030
corporately in in each of their boardrooms
and they're going to say hey let's do the

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:05.120
math on this if we open you know theatrically
for six to eight weeks pre vod what will the

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.709
numbers look like if we go day and date what
will the numbers look like i mean it's all

00:03:08.709 --> 00:03:13.730
about numbers right i mean they're in business
to make money and they got to decide what

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.739
is the best way to you know to cater to their
audience so that they can make the revenue

00:03:18.739 --> 00:03:24.030
they want to make so i i really don't know
um i know the theaters i don't know for sure

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:28.329
but what i've read is the theaters obviously
are going to want an exclusive window you

00:03:28.329 --> 00:03:36.549
know pre the vod release but maybe the studios
have learned something new that may carry

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:41.739
on and frankly i mean as a consumer that's
not going to matter to me i don't really care

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:45.820
whether there's a date vod release with the
theatrical release i'll still go to the movie

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:50.109
theater to see the movie because i want to
see it that way and i will pay my 15 and buy

00:03:50.109 --> 00:03:54.049
my popcorn and everything because i want to
go out i mean i want to i want to watch those

00:03:54.049 --> 00:04:00.180
big movies that way so even if they're offered
at the same time you know on vod i'm still

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:05.790
not going i'd rather consume it that way now
if they're not and that's a way to get subscribers

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:11.409
by offering them day and date that's a corporate
decision and you know i'll understand but

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:16.530
i don't know movie theaters so if i did i
wouldn't understand how has the pandemic changed

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:22.130
your company and you as a filmmaker that's
a great question well my company did not make

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:27.600
a movie in 2020 we were supposed to i have
a great other new sports drama that i was

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.140
hoping to shoot and i've already kind of almost
set up pre-distribution for it so i was really

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:40.560
excited about it and but the pandemic slowed
that down because the protocols for shooting

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.670
were very very restrictive and plus personally
i don't really want to do that i mean i don't

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:49.070
i didn't want to put my own health at risk
i didn't want to put the crew or the cast

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:54.320
or anybody at risk so i didn't feel it was
worth it to to have to shoot during the pandemic

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.590
and it's still in you know it's still sort
of waiting um i'm hoping maybe we could shoot

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:04.530
this fall but uh we have to see how that all
plays out but um i'm respectful of the fact

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:10.660
that hey it's a pandemic crazy thing crazy
year crazy almost year and a half now by the

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:17.960
time this is over um but it happens and you
know we'll get through it and we'll see what

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:23.100
looks like on the other end so i'm anxious
to to shoot a game but i didn't want to shoot

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.860
during the pandemic which meant i had like
you know a year more little more than a year

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:36.180
to do other stuff so that actually was interesting
and you know at first i was kind of disappointed

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:42.570
but then i said let's make you know lemons
into lemonade here so i did two things one

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:47.460
is i stepped up my consulting practice quite
a bit so i do a lot more consulting with indie

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:53.060
filmmakers and you know i'm enjoying that
because everybody is kind of struggling and

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:57.810
going through the same thing and it's nice
to chat with people and give them advice and

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.440
i think i've connected with a lot of filmmakers
and i feel like they've learned a lot and

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.010
i've hopefully helped them through stuff and
i'm sure some of them will be watching this

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:12.730
interview and hopefully make nice comments
but also i started teaching too so i live

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:17.750
in san diego i teach at san diego state university
and i teach in the film production department

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:23.020
but i teach the business aspects of indie
filmmaking so it's primarily distribution

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.150
and finance which are the boring parts of
the film business you know the students want

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:32.020
to learn about camera and lighting acting
directing editing all that kind of stuff you

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:38.870
know film making right production so they
have to take my course to graduate if you're

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.910
if you're a film major and they learn about
finance and distribution primarily a little

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:49.980
bit about budgeting and scheduling and so
it's been interesting because at first the

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:55.210
students are you know a little leery of it
you know because they don't realize what that

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:00.950
entails but i've really gotten great feedback
on that from all the students who are taking

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:06.110
my course and they're primarily upper level
students because they didn't really understand

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:11.120
distribution at all i mean they've heard the
word but they had no clue what it is i talk

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.280
a lot about marketing and you know how to
kind of make sure that you've got your plans

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:20.670
in place before you start shooting so that
maybe you'll do things differently during

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:26.260
your shoot if you need to um and you know
the whole financing piece everybody's really

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:32.360
curious about how that is all gets put together
there's legal terms technical terms lots of

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:38.800
business terms which yeah people who are artists
and filmmakers don't really love that but

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.630
totally appreciate it once they understand
how it all fits together to become you know

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:50.210
the film business and so in my course i call
it the business of film um the students call

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:53.810
it the film business i always say you should
put the business part first not the film part

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:57.550
but you know they're both equal i mean you
need to have the art obviously the artistic

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:03.150
side but they're taking you know a dozen courses
on that side and one course in business and

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:08.080
you know maybe that will expand into more
i believe in order to be a successful filmmaker

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.950
as i've said many times in your interviews
and to other people it's really helpful to

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:17.980
know what really goes on on the business side
primarily the distribution side you don't

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:22.310
have to do it all and i tell people you don't
have to become an expert in it but it's good

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:27.580
to understand it to at least be aware what
goes on what other people do so that you you

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.519
know you can be part of the conversation and
the narrative and and just be a little bit

00:08:32.519 --> 00:08:37.639
more up to speed so that when you do cross
those bridges at least you know what the bridge

00:08:37.639 --> 00:08:42.669
is what do you start out with once you get
through all of sort of the the housekeeping

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:47.610
of the of the course and and the timeline
and what materials they need what do you start

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:53.060
with so it's interesting um so i teach in
my course which is the business aspects of

00:08:53.060 --> 00:09:00.160
filmmaking i teach three units i teach distribution
production and finance and i actually teach

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.660
them in that order so inevitably the first
question i get asked is why are you teaching

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:10.110
distribution before you teach production like
shouldn't you be teaching you know all the

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:15.210
legal and business aspects of making a movie
which is production before you teach selling

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:19.920
a movie or how about you shouldn't you teach
financing a movie before you teach selling

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:27.670
or making a movie but i chose those that that
order on purpose because i said in order to

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:32.350
be a successful filmmaker because i you know
i teach about sort of commercially viable

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:37.410
filmmaking obviously you have to be a good
artist you have to make a good film you can't

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:41.829
sell a bad film so i take for granted i say
i assume you're going to make a good film

00:09:41.829 --> 00:09:46.769
that's what the other teachers are teaching
at you how to make a great film so i'll check

00:09:46.769 --> 00:09:52.639
that box but before you make that great film
you really need to understand you know three

00:09:52.639 --> 00:09:56.810
major things who your audience is who you're
making it for you know how you're going to

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:00.850
create awareness with that audience and will
your audience pay to consume it if you want

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:06.029
to make money with your movies and that's
all distribution so it's really important

00:10:06.029 --> 00:10:12.759
to understand those components before you
make your film because one is you might make

00:10:12.759 --> 00:10:17.120
a different film two is you might not make
the film you're making i mean if you really

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.519
want to look at the business component so
that like at the end you don't struggle and

00:10:21.519 --> 00:10:27.192
say oh if i only would have known or if i
had a thought of that first that's why i teach

00:10:27.192 --> 00:10:32.610
distribution first because distribution really
is enlightening to understand sort of the

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:36.370
film that you're crafting so that you make
the right film so you could sell it afterwards

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:40.891
and and even while you're making it you have
to understand distribution so that maybe you

00:10:40.891 --> 00:10:44.870
have you're making components that help you
market the film or maybe even doing social

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.339
media from set or that type of thing you're
marketing marketing marketing preparing to

00:10:49.339 --> 00:10:53.990
sell your film before you make it have you
been surprised by any of the questions or

00:10:53.990 --> 00:11:00.480
actually just pleasantly sort of enlightened
by how advanced some of these individuals

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:07.230
are when it comes to distribution yeah well
no i mean all i'm going to say ninety-nine

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:11.990
i say probably 100 of students who go to film
school go to learn production i mean that's

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:18.410
what film school is teaching you how to make
a movie i think our school San Diego State

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:24.741
University [SDSU] recognized that it's really
important to teach distribution of finance

00:11:24.741 --> 00:11:29.699
because those are such important components
of being a successful career filmmaker but

00:11:29.699 --> 00:11:33.850
for the most part students don't expect to
learn about those kind of things because those

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:39.949
are sort of the business things and film schools
about the artistic things so at first i'm

00:11:39.949 --> 00:11:44.149
not surprised they're surprised the students
are surprised when they take the course they

00:11:44.149 --> 00:11:47.960
have to take my course to graduate if you're
a film major so it's a prereq you know there's

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:52.300
two prerequisites and you know and it's a
mandatory course so you know i'm the course

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:56.369
they don't want to take because it's all the
boring stuff it's legal business stuff that

00:11:56.369 --> 00:12:01.579
type of thing but once they do take it and
they start understanding about you know how

00:12:01.579 --> 00:12:06.360
distribution works and what the different
platforms are and how they buy and sell and

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:11.170
and how movies you know get financed and this
kind of thing they're totally intrigued by

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:18.279
it they're probably a little overwhelmed by
it as well because there's so much that they

00:12:18.279 --> 00:12:24.779
haven't really thought about you know financing
a movie is in my estimation far more complicated

00:12:24.779 --> 00:12:29.879
than making the movie or editing it um like
i say everything's important i'm not belittling

00:12:29.879 --> 00:12:36.329
any everything anything but um to understand
you know how the whole system works from start

00:12:36.329 --> 00:12:43.879
to finish as a business is fascinating for
them and and they're learning a lot they're

00:12:43.879 --> 00:12:47.800
learning things that they had no idea they
were going to learn about so i'm really happy

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:52.420
with how much they're being exposed to and
learning but they're also there's lots and

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:57.249
lots of questions because once you learn a
little bit you want to kind of learn more

00:12:57.249 --> 00:13:00.540
and there's just not enough time to answer
it all as a matter of fact i've had to cut

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:06.120
my materials in half twice because there's
just so much to teach and and i take for granted

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:09.930
because i know it all you know that they can
i can go quicker but but i have to go a little

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:16.970
slower because you know i just throw around
terms you know like v o d s no big deal right

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:21.800
like that's if you're in the movie business
you know about that you don't know about that

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:26.149
if you're a film student you don't know what
those platforms are you've heard of netflix

00:13:26.149 --> 00:13:29.850
you know you've heard of disney plus you've
heard of some of these things but you don't

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:34.079
necessarily know what all these things are
so i have to really take time to explain all

00:13:34.079 --> 00:13:38.649
that kind of stuff and it's important because
now they have a very good handle on potentially

00:13:38.649 --> 00:13:43.449
where the movies they're gonna make are gonna
go and of course it's gonna change you know

00:13:43.449 --> 00:13:44.952
probably changes all the time but for now.

00:13:44.952 --> 00:13:44.966
Questions For The Viewers: What did you like
about this video?

